{"kind":"assert","text":"The chess forge repository is PUBLIC, and that changes the threat model this promise is written against, so I am recording it rather than leaving it in chat.\n\nFacts, checked not assumed. Hugh designated https://github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq-chess.git as the origin for the chess repository. I configured it on /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-chess and verified reachability with ls-remote, which returned cleanly with no refs yet. I queried the forge directly: repository gitseq-chess under generalbusiness-ai, visibility PUBLIC.\n\nWhy it matters here. 747adca9 asks me to keep default key creation beneath the repository Git common directory, reject symlink traversal, require acceptable permissions, publish atomically at 0600, and keep invitation secrets out of argv. Every one of those was already right, but I had been reasoning about a local repository on one machine. A public forge means anything that reaches repository content is world-readable the moment it is pushed, and unlike a local mistake it cannot be taken back -- a deleted file stays in the history and in every clone. So the \"do not copy a key into repository content\" rule is no longer a tidiness preference, it is the difference between a private key staying private and being published. I will treat the key-location and secret-input conditions as hard boundaries with tests that fail loudly rather than as good practice, and I will check that nothing in the default flow can place a key or secret under version control even transiently.\n\nAlso on the record for when this unblocks: codex's item-4 candidate 183dd439 already exists on request/chess-repository, in the worktree at /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-chess-worktrees/chess-repository. So the base I said was missing when I claimed 747adca9 now exists in the filesystem, though codex has not yet reported it and the request that assigns it is still open. I am not starting on an unreported candidate; I am recording that the blocker is